    {
      "type": "worked_example",
      "id": "335a5d2d-1d5a-52df-9a3d-c43ee05353a2",
      "citations": [
        {
          "source_id": "8f4eb7d8-fdc9-4966-827e-880bca61bef1",
          "version": 1,
          "chunk_id": "02c18827-395e-43f0-b70a-009d0b7ce324"
        }
      ],
      "title": "A LUT stores a truth table",
      "prompt": "What four stored bits implement XOR in a 2-input LUT addressed by AB=00,01,10,11?",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "Evaluate XOR at each address.",
          "latex": "0,1,1,0"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "Store 0,1,1,0 in address order. A k-input LUT needs $2^k$ truth-table entries."
    },
    {
      "type": "worked_example",
      "id": "29e301ee-338d-56f5-929a-123bab4e6ce8",
      "citations": [
        {
          "source_id": "8f4eb7d8-fdc9-4966-827e-880bca61bef1",
          "version": 1,
          "chunk_id": "02c18827-395e-43f0-b70a-009d0b7ce324"
        }
      ],
      "title": "PLA product terms can be shared",
      "prompt": "For $F=AB+C$ and $G=AB+D$, give the product terms and output connections.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "Create product lines P1=AB, P2=C, P3=D.",
          "latex": ""
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Select product lines for each OR output.",
          "latex": "F=P_1+P_2,\\quad G=P_1+P_3"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "The AB term is generated once and used twice. This illustrates the programmable AND–OR structure."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "630804c9-7805-5d49-ac17-3b112bfef2f9",
      "citations": [
        {
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1,
          "chunk_id": "b1fe982d-865e-4d16-8117-3aef88f19ee0"
        }
      ],
      "text": "**Propagation delay** is the time between an input transition and the resulting output transition. **Fan-in** counts a gate’s inputs; **fan-out** describes the load it drives. More loading can increase delay, so fewer gates need not mean the fastest circuit."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "f5b61a8f-233a-5d34-97f3-dccc9bd45890",
      "citations": [
        {
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1,
          "chunk_id": "b1fe982d-865e-4d16-8117-3aef88f19ee0"
        }
      ],
      "text": "A buffer reproduces its input and can provide drive. A **tri-state buffer** drives 0 or 1 when enabled and disconnects its output when disabled; that disconnected state is **Z**, not logic 0. A shared bus needs controls that prevent conflicting drivers."
    },
    {
      "type": "practice_problem",
      "id": "be32797e-bfe1-5c8d-abc3-e4f3898e0240",
      "citations": [],
      "title": "Try it: tri-state bus control",
      "prompt": "Driver A drives 1 and driver B drives 0 onto the same bus. Is the bus value an OR of their outputs?",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "Both outputs actively try to impose different voltages. Boolean OR does not model this connection.",
          "latex": ""
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Disable one driver to Z before allowing the other to drive.",
          "latex": ""
        }
      ],
      "answer": "No; the bus has contention. With both drivers disabled, the bus is undriven unless another circuit establishes its level.",
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "origin": "original"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "a1fcd743-9ec3-5408-9166-10a5d5b24e2f",
      "citations": [
        {
